What the translation covers:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdriver(const char *in)[0m and [38;5;10mfoo(const char *in, char c)[0m — both are non-static in the C, so both are real exported symbols in the shared object. There are no namespace-renaming macros in [38;5;10mdriver.h[0m, so the linker names are plain [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mfoo[0m, confirmed with [38;5;10mnm[0m.[0m[0m
- The odd [38;5;10mfor (const char *s = in; s = strchr(s, c); s++)[0m loop is reproduced literally, including the post-match [38;5;10ms++[0m. I wrote a local [38;5;10mstrchr[0m with C semantics rather than using [38;5;10mCStr[0m, so [38;5;10mc == 0[0m still matches the terminator and walks past it exactly as the C does — that's latent UB in the original, and I preserved it instead of guarding against it.[0m[0m
- Output goes through the platform [38;5;10mprintf[0m rather than Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m, so formatting and stdio buffering behavior match the C library exactly. This also matters if a caller mixes its own C stdio writes with calls into this library.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing worth flagging: [38;5;10mfoo[0m takes [38;5;10mchar[0m, which is signed on x86-64 Linux, so I mapped it to [38;5;10mc_char[0m. On a platform where [38;5;10mchar[0m is unsigned (e.g. aarch64 Linux), [38;5;10mc_char[0m follows the platform, so the signature stays correct there too.[0m[0m
